
#include <stdio.h>

int main()
{
    int a[100], b[100], c[200];
    int n, m, i = 0, j = 0, k = 0;

    printf("Enter number of students in Campus 1: ");
    scanf("%d", &n);

    printf("Enter Campus 1 scores in descending order:\n");
    for(i = 0; i < n; i++)
        scanf("%d", &a[i]);

    printf("Enter number of students in Campus 2: ");
    scanf("%d", &m);

    printf("Enter Campus 2 scores in descending order:\n");
    for(j = 0; j < m; j++)
        scanf("%d", &b[j]);

    i = 0;
    j = 0;

    // Merge two sorted arrays
    while(i < n && j < m)
    {
        if(a[i] >= b[j])
            c[k++] = a[i++];
        else
            c[k++] = b[j++];
    }

    while(i < n)
        c[k++] = a[i++];

    while(j < m)
        c[k++] = b[j++];

    printf("Combined merit list:\n");

    for(i = 0; i < k; i++)
        printf("%d ", c[i]);

    return 0;
}
Pseudocode
1. Start.
2. Read the number of students in Campus 1 and Campus 2.
3. Read both arrays in descending order.
4. Initialize i = 0, j = 0, k = 0.
5. Repeat while both arrays have elements:
   - If a[i] >= b[j], copy a[i] into c[k].
   - Otherwise, copy b[j] into c[k].
   - Increment the corresponding index and k.
6. Copy remaining elements from Campus 1.
7. Copy remaining elements from Campus 2.
8. Display the combined merit list.
9. Stop.

